Main Types of Data

• Quantitative Data: Numerical and measurable facts (e.g., age, height, temperature, or financial metrics).
• Qualitative Data: Descriptive and non-numerical information (e.g., text, colors, names, or audio recordings).
• Structured Data: Highly organized information formatted into neat rows and columns, such as relational databases or Microsoft Excel spreadsheets.
• Unstructured Data: Free-form or multimedia data that lacks a predefined model, such as video files, images, or natural text.
• Semi-Structured Data: Data containing organizational markers or tags like JSON or XML files.

Key Uses and Importance

• Decision-Making: Transforms raw observations into actionable business intelligence and operational strategies.
• Artificial Intelligence: Serves as the foundational training material for machine learning models and generative AI systems.
• Research and Science: Enables empirical testing, experimentation tracking, and predictive modeling across medicine, meteorology, and engineering.
